This repository is not single-licensed. Different components carry different
licenses, matching their role in the project rather than a single blanket
choice. The rule of thumb: the client/capture layer is permissive, the
hosted-coordination layer is copyleft.

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
Component: packages/cli, packages/daemon, hook/ (twing-hook)
License:   MIT OR Apache-2.0 (your choice)
Why:       This is the "git" layer -- the more freely it spreads (bundled by
           orchestrators, embedded anywhere, zero legal friction), the
           better, and it isn't what's being monetized.
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
Component: packages/core (shared types, wire schemas, Tree-sitter wrapper)
License:   MIT OR Apache-2.0 (your choice)
Why:       Infrastructure both sides depend on; keeping it permissive means
           the client never has any AGPL entanglement.
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
Component: packages/server (twing serve)
License:   AGPL-3.0-only
Why:       The one codebase that is the future managed product. The
           network-use clause means: self-host it unmodified for your own
           team, zero obligation -- exactly today's usage pattern. Fork it,
           modify it, and offer it as your own hosted service to other
           users -- the scenario that matters -- and you owe the source back.
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

Full license texts are at the repository root:
  LICENSE-MIT     -- MIT License
  LICENSE-APACHE  -- Apache License, Version 2.0
  LICENSE-AGPL    -- GNU Affero General Public License, Version 3

Each component directory carries its own short LICENSE file pointing back to
the applicable text(s) above, and each package's package.json `license`
field records the same choice as an SPDX expression.
